1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,664
This is ECE 320, video 12. We solve the pipeline design questions of two past midterms.

2
00:00:07,114 --> 00:00:19,746
After this video, you can add new instructions and new registers to the pipeline. You can judge a change to the branch hardware. And you can find the cost of a code pattern when M has a cache, and remove that cost.

3
00:00:20,196 --> 00:00:32,920
A design question asks you to change the pipeline. Use the same six questions every time. First, the new state. Which new registers hold the new values? In which stage are they read and written?

4
00:00:33,370 --> 00:00:39,872
Second, the paths and the muxes. Which wires carry the new values? Which mux selects them?

5
00:00:40,322 --> 00:00:45,802
Third, the control signals. The decoder sets new signals for each new instruction.

6
00:00:46,252 --> 00:00:53,961
Fourth, the hazards. Find the new read after write cases. Then decide: forward by MX or WX, or stall.

7
00:00:54,411 --> 00:01:01,005
Fifth, precise state. Write new state in W, after M. M is the commit point.

8
00:01:01,455 --> 00:01:11,858
Sixth, run a program on the new hardware. Fill the pipeline diagram and count the cycles. Each of these six parts can give marks, so name every new register, mux and signal.

9
00:01:12,308 --> 00:01:25,961
Here is question 4 of the fall 2019 midterm. Homer adds two 32-bit registers, HI and LO, in the decode stage. He also adds three new instructions.

10
00:01:26,411 --> 00:01:33,470
W mul multiplies register rs1 by register rs2. The product has 64 bits.

11
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:39,168
W mul writes the upper 32 bits into HI, and the lower 32 bits into LO.

12
00:01:39,618 --> 00:01:47,725
Move from HI copies HI into register rd. Move from LO copies LO into register rd.

13
00:01:48,175 --> 00:01:52,633
The question asks for all the necessary changes. Pause the video and try it.

14
00:01:53,083 --> 00:02:14,028
Start with one row per instruction. W mul reads rs1 and rs2 in D, from the register file. In X, a multiplier makes the 64-bit product. We assume that it takes one X cycle. The X M and M W registers get 32 more bits. In W, W mul writes HI and LO.

15
00:02:14,478 --> 00:02:28,688
Move from HI and move from LO read HI or LO in D. A new mux selects the value. The ALU passes the value to the output. In W, the instruction writes rd, as any ALU instruction does.

16
00:02:29,138 --> 00:02:34,665
So treat HI and LO like the register file. Read them in D, and write them in W.

17
00:02:35,115 --> 00:02:52,251
Why write them in W, and not at the end of X? An older instruction can still raise an exception in M. Then the handler would see a result of a younger instruction. That state is not precise. W comes after M, the commit point. So a write in W keeps the state precise.

18
00:02:52,701 --> 00:03:01,039
Here is the class pipeline with forwarding, in gray. The green and blue lines are the MX and WX paths into the ALU input A.

19
00:03:01,489 --> 00:03:05,994
Change one. Add the two new registers, HI and LO, in D.

20
00:03:06,444 --> 00:03:22,930
Change two. Add a mux in D, before the D X register. We call its select signal H L Sel. It picks rs1, HI or LO for the ALU input A. Move from HI and move from LO use this path.

21
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:34,479
Change three. Extend the ALU with a multiplier. Its result has 64 bits. So the X M and M W registers each get 32 more bits, in orange.

22
00:03:34,929 --> 00:03:53,134
Change four. In W, the 64-bit value of the M W register splits. Bits 31 to 0 go to LO, and bits 63 to 32 go to HI. A new write enable, H L W En, controls this write. It is 1 only for W mul.

23
00:03:53,584 --> 00:04:02,268
Change five. The forwarding mux of input A must give the right half. Move from LO takes the lower half. Move from HI takes the upper half.

24
00:04:02,718 --> 00:04:21,666
Now the decoder. W mul sets H L Sel to rs1, so the ALU input A gets the rs1 value. A L U Sel selects multiply. H L W En is 1, so W writes HI and LO. Reg W En is 0, because W mul writes no x register.

25
00:04:22,116 --> 00:04:32,007
Move from HI sets H L Sel to HI. The ALU passes input A to its output. Reg W En is 1, and W B Sel selects the ALU.

26
00:04:32,457 --> 00:04:36,405
Move from LO is the same, but H L Sel selects LO.

27
00:04:36,855 --> 00:04:50,926
All other instructions set H L Sel to rs1 and H L W En to 0. Their other signals do not change. The names H L Sel and H L W En are our names. The question gives no names.

28
00:04:51,376 --> 00:05:04,054
Next, the hazards. Only W mul writes HI and LO, in W. Move from HI and move from LO read them in D. So the forwarding unit compares opcodes. It does not need to compare register numbers.

29
00:05:04,504 --> 00:05:16,393
Case one, MX. In this short run, move from HI, number 2, is in X in cycle 4. W mul is in M. So mux A selects the X M register.

30
00:05:16,843 --> 00:05:35,604
Case two, WX. Here, move from LO, number 3, is in X in cycle 5, and W mul is in W. The instruction in M is not a W mul, because the most recent value wins. So mux A selects the M W register.

31
00:05:36,054 --> 00:05:50,820
Case three. Here, move from HI, number 4, is in D in cycle 5, and W mul is in W. There is no forwarding into D, so D reads the old value. Number 4 stalls one cycle. This is the W D case.

32
00:05:51,270 --> 00:06:02,228
The x registers need no change. W mul reads rs1 and rs2, and the move instructions write rd. The class forwarding and stall logic handles these.

33
00:06:02,678 --> 00:06:08,994
Part B asks you to run this sequence on the new pipeline. Use arrows for forwarding. Pause the video and try it.

34
00:06:09,444 --> 00:06:16,271
Number 1, W mul, multiplies in X, in cycle 3. The product exists at the end of cycle 3.

35
00:06:16,721 --> 00:06:25,126
Number 2, move from LO, is in X in cycle 4. W mul is in M. So LO goes by MX.

36
00:06:25,576 --> 00:06:35,700
Number 3, move from HI, is in X in cycle 5. W mul is in W. So HI goes by WX.

37
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:49,154
Number 4, move from LO, is in D in cycle 5. W mul writes LO in the same cycle. D reads the old LO, so D repeats. Number 5 repeats F.

38
00:06:49,604 --> 00:07:00,981
In cycle 6, number 4 reads the new LO. In cycle 7, the store reads x 6 in D. Number 2 wrote x 6 in cycle 6, so the register file has it.

39
00:07:01,431 --> 00:07:06,725
The sequence takes 10 cycles. There is one stall cycle in D, and one in F.

40
00:07:07,175 --> 00:07:22,547
Here are the two tables. Number 1 forwards LO to number 2, from M to X, in cycle 4. Number 1 forwards HI to number 3, from W to X, in cycle 5. Green rings mark both cells.

41
00:07:22,997 --> 00:07:32,889
Stall table. Number 4 stalls in D in cycle 5. W mul writes LO in W in the same cycle, and D reads the old LO.

42
00:07:33,339 --> 00:07:45,181
Number 5 stalls in F in cycle 5, because number 4 holds D. This answer follows the model design of Part A. With a slower multiplier, the cycles change, so state your assumption.

43
00:07:45,631 --> 00:08:00,352
Part C. Homer removes the branch compare unit from X. The ALU compares instead, and a new adder computes the target. Here is the X stage of the class pipeline. The ALU computes the target, PC plus immediate.

44
00:08:00,802 --> 00:08:13,295
Look at the inputs of the branch compare unit. They come from the D X register, before the forwarding muxes. So the compare unit gets no forwarded values. The branch must read its operands in D, after the producer's W.

45
00:08:13,745 --> 00:08:29,999
In Homer's design, the ALU compares the two operands. It gives B R equal and B R less than. Its inputs come after the forwarding muxes. So the branch gets its operands by MX or WX, with no new paths.

46
00:08:30,449 --> 00:08:38,065
The ALU is now busy with the compare. So a new adder computes the target, PC plus immediate, in X at the same time.

47
00:08:38,515 --> 00:08:44,506
The branch still resolves in X. Pause the video and try it: how many stall cycles does Homer save?

48
00:08:44,956 --> 00:09:01,349
Here is a branch right after its producer, in the class pipeline. B E Q reads x 1 in D. add writes x 1 in W in cycle 5. So B E Q stalls 3 cycles and reads x 1 in cycle 6.

49
00:09:01,799 --> 00:09:13,780
In Homer's design, B E Q is in X in cycle 4, and add is in M. So x 1 goes by MX. There is no stall. The run goes from 11 to 8 cycles.

50
00:09:14,230 --> 00:09:27,187
With a load as the producer, the class pipeline also stalls 3 cycles. Homer's design stalls one cycle, the load-use case, and then uses WX. The run goes from 11 to 9 cycles.

51
00:09:27,637 --> 00:09:45,284
Here is a taken branch in Homer's design. It resolves in X, in cycle 4. The two younger instructions are flushed, so they show dashes. The answer for Part C: Homer removes the stalls of a branch that uses a recent result. The penalty of a wrong guess stays 2 cycles.

52
00:09:45,734 --> 00:10:03,103
Now question 4 of the fall 2022 paper. It uses the class pipeline with forwarding. There is no forwarding into D, and no forwarding into the branch compare unit. A C L S D pattern is a sequence of consecutive loads that write the same destination register.

53
00:10:03,553 --> 00:10:12,980
In program P, numbers 1 and 2 both load into x 1. This is a write after write pair. Nobody reads the x 1 value of number 1.

54
00:10:13,430 --> 00:10:18,260
Number 4 reads x 1. Its producer is number 2, the latest writer of x 1.

55
00:10:18,710 --> 00:10:24,793
Part A asks you to execute program P and explain every stall. Pause the video and try it.

56
00:10:25,243 --> 00:10:31,606
The four instructions enter one per cycle. Number 1 computes its address in X, in cycle 3.

57
00:10:32,056 --> 00:10:44,130
Number 1 reads D mem in M, in cycle 4. Its value exists at the end of cycle 4. Number 3 reads x 3, and no instruction before it writes x 3.

58
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:54,240
Number 4 is in X in cycle 6. Number 2, the latest writer of x 1, is in W. So x 1 goes by WX. There is no stall.

59
00:10:54,690 --> 00:11:02,677
Program P takes 8 cycles with no stalls. So the stall explanation is: there are none. The work of number 1 is wasted.

60
00:11:03,127 --> 00:11:17,384
Part B adds a cache in M. The data memory is a 2-way set associative cache with 16 sets and 16 byte blocks. It uses L R U replacement. A larger age means that the block was used less recently.

61
00:11:17,834 --> 00:11:27,680
M stalls on a miss. A hit takes 1 cycle. A miss with a vacant way takes 2 cycles. A miss in a full set evicts a block and takes 4 cycles.

62
00:11:28,130 --> 00:11:37,928
Here is the cache at the start, with the first four sets. Set 1 is marked. x 4 holds hex 410, and x 5 holds hex 610.

63
00:11:38,378 --> 00:11:47,666
Part B asks for the pipeline diagram, the final registers and cache, and the M latency of numbers 1 and 2. Pause the video and try it.

64
00:11:48,116 --> 00:11:57,544
First, split each address. A 16 byte block needs 4 offset bits. 16 sets need 4 index bits. The tag is the other 24 bits.

65
00:11:57,994 --> 00:12:07,375
Number 1 loads from hex 410. The offset is 0. The index bits are 0 0 0 1, so set 1. The tag is hex 4.

66
00:12:07,825 --> 00:12:14,326
Number 2 loads from hex 610. This is also set 1, with tag hex 6.

67
00:12:14,776 --> 00:12:27,408
So both loads use set 1. Here is set 1 of the given cache. It holds tag hex 6 in way 0, and tag hex 8 in way 1. The block of number 2 is already in the cache.

68
00:12:27,858 --> 00:12:36,171
Now run the two loads through set 1. At the start, way 0 has age 1, so way 0 is the least recently used block.

69
00:12:36,621 --> 00:12:48,834
Number 1 looks for tag hex 4. No way matches, so it is a miss. The set is full. L R U picks way 0, the larger age. Tag hex 6 is evicted. The latency is 4 cycles.

70
00:12:49,284 --> 00:13:02,720
Then the ages change. The slide rule: the used block gets age 0. A block with a lower age than its old age gets plus 1. So way 0 gets age 0, and way 1 gets age 1.

71
00:13:03,170 --> 00:13:14,687
Number 2 looks for tag hex 6. Number 1 just evicted it, so this is a miss too. Way 1 now has the larger age. Tag hex 8 is evicted. The latency is again 4 cycles.

72
00:13:15,137 --> 00:13:21,267
This is the important point. The dead load, number 1, evicted the block that number 2 needs.

73
00:13:21,717 --> 00:13:31,702
Number 1 misses in a full set, so M takes 4 cycles. It stays in M for cycles 4 to 7. M is busy, so number 2 waits in X.

74
00:13:32,152 --> 00:13:42,229
Every younger instruction holds its stage. Number 3 repeats D, and number 4 repeats F. As usual, every repeat except the last gets a star.

75
00:13:42,679 --> 00:13:55,729
Number 1 writes x 1 in W, in cycle 8. Number 2 enters M in cycle 8. It also misses in a full set. It stays in M for cycles 8 to 11.

76
00:13:56,179 --> 00:14:06,163
Number 2 writes x 1 in W in cycle 12. Number 4 is in X in cycle 12. So x 1 goes by WX, with no extra stall.

77
00:14:06,613 --> 00:14:15,576
Program P now takes 14 cycles, 6 more than in Part A. The latency in M is 4 cycles for number 1, and 4 cycles for number 2.

78
00:14:16,026 --> 00:14:29,651
Here are the final registers. x 1 holds the word from address hex 610, the value of number 2. x 3 is hex 2 F 1. x 6 is that word plus 1. The other registers do not change.

79
00:14:30,101 --> 00:14:41,293
Here is the final cache. Only set 1 changes. Way 0 holds tag hex 4 with age 1. Way 1 holds tag hex 6 with age 0.

80
00:14:41,743 --> 00:14:52,424
The latencies. Number 1 takes 4 cycles in M: a miss in a full set. Number 2 also takes 4 cycles. It misses, because number 1 evicted its block.

81
00:14:52,874 --> 00:15:00,816
The exam gives no memory values. So write x 1 as the memory word at hex 610, and x 6 as that word plus 1.

82
00:15:01,266 --> 00:15:15,151
Part C asks for the side effects. Side effect one: number 1 uses M for a value that nobody reads. Its miss takes 4 cycles, 3 more than a hit. So it adds 3 stall cycles. This degrades performance.

83
00:15:15,601 --> 00:15:27,397
Side effect two: number 1 changes the cache. Here, it evicts the block that number 2 needs. So number 2 misses too, and adds 3 more stall cycles. This also degrades performance.

84
00:15:27,847 --> 00:15:36,578
Side effect three: in another program, the block of number 1 can be useful. A later load to that block then hits. This benefits performance.

85
00:15:37,028 --> 00:15:45,851
If number 1 does not access the cache, number 2 hits. Program P then takes 8 cycles, not 8 plus 3 plus 3, which is 14.

86
00:15:46,301 --> 00:16:02,277
Part D asks for a change to the control. Look at cycle 3. Number 2 is in D, in the F D register. Number 1 is in X, in the D X register. The class pipeline already has a kill or no op mux before D X, for stalls.

87
00:16:02,727 --> 00:16:13,036
Add a new check to the hazard unit. The instruction in X is a load. The instruction in D is a load. They have the same rd. And the load in D does not read that rd.

88
00:16:13,486 --> 00:16:23,130
If all are true, the hazard unit sets a kill signal. A new mux before the X M register selects a no op. A no op is an instruction that does nothing.

89
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:32,450
In cycle 4, M holds the no op. It does not access the cache, and it writes no register in W. So number 1 shows dashes in M and W.

90
00:16:32,900 --> 00:16:42,861
Do not kill number 1 if number 2 reads rd. For example, load word x 1, 0 of x 1 needs the x 1 value of number 1.

91
00:16:43,311 --> 00:16:51,573
One more caution. If number 1 had a bad address, it would raise an exception. The kill removes that exception. Mention this in the exam.

92
00:16:52,023 --> 00:17:00,150
Here is program P with the kill. In cycle 3, the hazard unit sees two loads to x 1. It kills number 1, which is in X.

93
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:04,780
Number 1 shows dashes in M and W. It does not access the cache.

94
00:17:05,230 --> 00:17:18,465
Number 2 accesses set 1 in cycle 5. Its tag, hex 6, is still in way 0, so this is a hit. M takes 1 cycle. Way 0 gets age 0, and way 1 gets age 1.

95
00:17:18,915 --> 00:17:24,488
Number 4 gets x 1 from number 2 by WX in cycle 6, as in Part A.

96
00:17:24,938 --> 00:17:34,863
Program P takes 8 cycles, 6 fewer than with the dead load. The registers end with the same values. So yes, the pipeline can improve the C L S D pattern.

97
00:17:35,313 --> 00:17:46,087
Here is the recap. One. Answer a design question with a list: new state, paths and muxes, control signals, hazards, precise state, and a run.

98
00:17:46,537 --> 00:17:57,652
Two. HI and LO are read in D and written in W. Add a mux in D and a multiplier in X. Widen X M and M W by 32 bits.

99
00:17:58,102 --> 00:18:09,712
Three. A read of HI or LO uses MX, WX, or a one-cycle W D stall. The check compares opcodes, because only W mul writes HI and LO.

100
00:18:10,162 --> 00:18:17,267
Four. With the compare in the ALU, a branch gets forwarded values. Three stalls become zero, or one after a load.

101
00:18:17,717 --> 00:18:23,429
Five. The wrong-guess penalty stays 2 cycles, because the branch still resolves in X.

102
00:18:23,879 --> 00:18:30,892
Six. In the C L S D pattern, the dead load misses and evicts the block of the next load. Program P takes 14 cycles.

103
00:18:31,342 --> 00:18:41,094
Seven. The fix: detect two loads to the same rd in D and X, and turn the older load into a no op. That is the end of video 12.
